                        "result_md": "\nStatue of Liberty & Ellis Island: The ultimate symbol of freedom. Take the ferry out to Liberty Island to see her up close, or jump on the free Staten Island Ferry for a fantastic, budget-friendly view of the skyline and monument. ([YouTube][1]) ([Capture'n Crave][2]) ([Instagram][3])  \n\nEmpire State Building: This historic 1931 Art Deco masterpiece offers panoramic views from its 86th and 102nd floors. If you prefer a view that actually includes the Empire State Building, check out SUMMIT One Vanderbilt or **Top of the Rock** . ([THE TRAVELING GINGER][4])  \n\n**Times Square** : Love it or hate it, the \"Crossroads of the World\" is a must-see sensory overload of flashing digital billboards, street performers, and theater marquees. It's also the gateway to catching a world-class Broadway show. ([NYC Tourism + Conventions][5])  \n\nBrooklyn Bridge: Walk across this iconic 1883 suspension bridge at sunrise or sunset for breathtaking views of Lower Manhattan. Once across, explore the trendy waterfront neighborhood of **DUMBO** . ([Instagram][3]) ([YouTube][6])  \n\n**Grand Central Terminal** : More than just a transit hub, this stunning architectural gem features a famous celestial ceiling, a bustling food hall, and the hidden \"Whispering Gallery\". ([bookish wayfarer][7])  \n\n\nCentral Park: Spanning 840 acres in the heart of Manhattan, this urban oasis features Bethesda Fountain, Sheep Meadow, Belvedere Castle, and miles of winding paths. Renting a bike is a highly recommended way to cover ground quickly.  \n\nThe High Line: A public park built on a historic, elevated freight rail line. It weaves nearly 1.5 miles through the West Side, offering unique perspectives of Chelsea’s architecture and the Hudson River. ([YouTube][6])  \n\n\nThe Metropolitan Museum of Art (The Met): The larges",
                        "result_text": "- Statue of Liberty & Ellis Island: The ultimate symbol of freedom. Take the ferry out to Liberty Island to see her up close, or jump on the free Staten Island Ferry for a fantastic, budget-friendly view of the skyline and monument.  \n- Empire State Building: This historic 1931 Art Deco masterpiece offers panoramic views from its 86th and 102nd floors. If you prefer a view that actually includes the Empire State Building, check out SUMMIT One Vanderbilt or **Top of the Rock** .  \n- **Times Square** : Love it or hate it, the \"Crossroads of the World\" is a must-see sensory overload of flashing digital billboards, street performers, and theater marquees. It's also the gateway to catching a world-class Broadway show.  \n- Brooklyn Bridge: Walk across this iconic 1883 suspension bridge at sunrise or sunset for breathtaking views of Lower Manhattan. Once across, explore the trendy waterfront neighborhood of **DUMBO** .  \n- **Grand Central Terminal** : More than just a transit hub, this stunning architectural gem features a famous celestial ceiling, a bustling food hall, and the hidden \"Whispering Gallery\".  \n\n- Central Park: Spanning 840 acres in the heart of Manhattan, this urban oasis features Bethesda Fountain, Sheep Meadow, Belvedere Castle, and miles of winding paths. Renting a bike is a highly recommended way to cover ground quickly.  \n- The High Line: A public park built on a historic, elevated freight rail line. It weaves nearly 1.5 miles through the West Side, offering unique perspectives of Chelsea’s architecture and the Hudson River.  \n\n- The Metropolitan Museum of Art (The Met): The largest art museum in the United States, housing over two million works spanning 5,000 years of global history, from ancient Egyptian temples to European masterpieces.  \n- Museum of Mod"
